digitwm is a scrollable-tiling window manager for X11, forked from cwm, the
calm window manager of the OpenBSD base system.

Windows live on an endless horizontal ribbon of columns; the screen is a
viewport onto that ribbon.  Opening a window pushes the ribbon along instead
of squeezing the neighbours into slivers, and moving focus scrolls the
viewport to the column you are working in.  The idea is the one niri brought
to Wayland; digitwm brings it to X11, where it runs on Linux, FreeBSD and
NetBSD alike.

It is not a compositor and does not animate.  Everything cwm does - groups,
menus, key bindings, floating windows - is still there, and a window can
leave the ribbon at any time.
